a-5ff5-baa5-03c536d5dd83'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Nelson headlines the Outlaw Music Festival; Mr. Erickson hits Brooklyn and Jersey City; and Mr. Clapton brings some Austin six-stringers to the Garden.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/01/arts/music/willie-nelson-roky-erickson-eric-clapton-outlaw-music-festival.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Nelson headlines the Outlaw Music Festival; Mr. Erickson hits Brooklyn and Jersey City; and Mr. Clapton brings some Austin six-stringers to the Garden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With all eyes on a hurricane-torn Texas, its perpetually touring musicians carry a spirit of resilience. Willie Nelson, despite recent respiratory problems, has been barnstorming across America with his encompassing repertoire of Americana. This weekend he headlines the Outlaw Music Festival of roots-rock and country in amphitheaters: Friday, Sept. 8, at PNC Bank Arts Center in Holmdel, N.J., and Saturday at Jones Beach Theater in Wantagh, N.Y. The lineup includes his son’s band, Lukas Nelson and Promise of the Real, along with Sheryl Crow, the Avett Brothers and Margo Price. The paladin of Texas psychedelia, Roky Erickson — who led the 13th Floor Elevators in the 1960s and returned, after some difficult years personally, with garage-rock visions of aliens, demons and redemption — performs in Brooklyn on Wednesday and Thursday at Rough Trade and Jersey City on Friday at White Eagle Hall. Meanwhile, Eric Clapton is bringing Austin bluesmen as openers — and rivals — when he plays Madison Square Garden on Thursday and Friday, sharing the bill with the incendiary guitarist Gary Clark Jr. and with Jimmie Vaughan, a founder of the Fabulous Thunderbirds.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/03/arts/03GUIDE5/03GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbS