ame': 'persons', 'value': 'Acosta, R Alexander', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-02T21:16:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc8367f5-7d96-54b8-bf3a-6a74b41d9f4c', 'word_count': 1109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc8367f5-7d96-54b8-bf3a-6a74b41d9f4c'}, {'abstract': 'Salvadoran women are facing an epidemic of violence fueled by impunity and a culture that normalizes machismo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/02/opinion/sunday/el-salvador-girls-homicides.html', 'snippet': 'Salvadoran women are facing an epidemic of violence fueled by impunity and a culture that normalizes machismo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN SALVADOR — I don’t want to go back to El Salvador. I felt afraid as a woman there more than in any other country in Latin America. I realized I had entered hostile territory while chatting with the taxi driver who picked me up at the airport, the first Salvadoran man I met. He told me he had a baby, a little darling called J. J., and showed me a photo.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/03/opinion/sunday/03lobo-guerrero/03lobo-guerrero-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/03/opinion/sunday/03lobo-guerrero/03lobo-guerrero-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/03/opinion/