 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2bc84cd-b3d2-5d5e-bb20-a8a1c22c1882', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2bc84cd-b3d2-5d5e-bb20-a8a1c22c1882'}, {'abstract': 'Rushdie’s 13th novel is exhausting, but it’s a treat when focused on a villain who resembles Donald Trump.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/04/books/review-golden-house-salman-rushdie.html', 'snippet': 'Rushdie’s 13th novel is exhausting, but it’s a treat when focused on a villain who resembles Donald Trump.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Salman Rushdie began work on his second novel, “Midnight’s Children” (1981), the one that would make his name, he realized, he has written, that he could not write his book “in cool Forsterian English. India was not cool. It was hot. It was hot and overcrowded and vulgar and loud and it needed a language to match that,” and he would try to find that language.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/books/05bookrushdie2/05bookrushdie2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/05/books/05bookrushdie2/05bookrushdie2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/books/05bookrushdie2/05bookrushdie2-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/books/05bookrushdie2/05bookrushdie2-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 838, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/05/books/05bookrushdie2/05bookrushdie2-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 838}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/books/05bookrushdie2/05bookrushdie2-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 734, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/books/05bookrushdie2/05bookrushdie2-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1260, 'width': 902, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Salman Rushdie’s Prose Joins the Circus in ‘The Golden House’', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Supervillain Gets Lost In the Sauce', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rushdie, Salman', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Golden House (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-04T16:56:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2729808-ff81-58b0-8401-d998f90da40d', 'word_count': 1008, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2729808-ff81-58b0-8401-d998f90da40d'}, {'abstract': 'With Steely Dan, he produced some of the most artistically and commercially potent music of the 1970s and early ’80s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/04/arts/music/walter-becker-steely-dan-best-songs.html', 'snippet': 'With Steely Dan, he produced some of the most artistically and commercially potent music of the 1970s and early ’80s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you think of Steely Dan, you probably hear Donald Fagen’s brackish snarl in the front of your mind. But Walter Becker, the group’s other half, played an equal role in defining its sound and direction. Mr. Becker, who died on Sunday at 67, founded Steely Dan — which was more of a protean studio project than a band with any set personnel — with Mr. Fagen, his perennial musical partner, in 1972. They sculpted a literary, restlessly inventive brand of jazz-rock, producing some of the most artistically and commercially potent music of that era. The group returned to touring in the 1990s, and released two warmly received albums in the new millennium. Here are 13 essential tracks that showcase Mr. Becker’s talents, including two from his low-profile solo career.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/arts/02beckerplaylist/02beckerplaylist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/05/arts/02beckerplaylist/02beckerplaylist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/arts/02beckerplaylist/02beckerplaylist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/arts/02beckerplaylist/02beckerplaylist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/05/arts/02beckerplaylist/02beckerplaylist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth