esk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/628e1cd4-5f08-5aad-87b6-ca609258d2de', 'word_count': 1172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/628e1cd4-5f08-5aad-87b6-ca609258d2de'}, {'abstract': 'Bad fiscal impact, higher chance of depressions. What’s not to like?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/09/05/the-very-bad-economics-of-killing-daca/', 'snippet': 'Bad fiscal impact, higher chance of depressions. What’s not to like?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trump’s decision to kill DACA — never mind the attempt to obscure things with that meaningless delay — is, first and foremost, a moral obscenity: throwing out 800,000 young people who are Americans in every way that matters, who have done nothing wrong, basically for racial reasons. But it’s also worth noting that Jeff Sessions just tried to sell it with junk economics, claiming that the Dreamers are taking American jobs. No, they aren’t, even if we leave aside the question of who’s an American. DACA is very much a boon to the rest of the U.S. population, and killing it will make everyone worse off.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/opinion/05krugmanWeb/05krugmanWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/05/opinion/05krugmanWeb/05krugmanWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/opinion/05krugmanWeb/05krugmanWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'wid