atoly Alexandrov, was a Communist who spent his life building the Soviet Union. He traveled a path from working as an unskilled laborer at a locomotive assembly plant in Rostov-on-Don, a large industrial city in southern Russia, to becoming the head of military transportations for the strategic North Caucasian Railroad.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/opinion/05edel3web/05edel3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/05/opinion/05edel3web/05edel3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/opinion/05edel3web/05edel3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/opinion/05edel3web/05edel3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 440, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/05/opinion/05edel3web/05edel3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 440}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/opinion/05edel3web/05edel3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/opinion/05edel3web/05edel3web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1501, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The ‘Bright Tomorrow’: Growing Up in the Brezhnev Era', 'kicker': 'Red Century', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Growing Up in the Brezhnev Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Revolution (1917)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Socialism (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'KGB', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brezhnev, Leonid I', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gorbachev, Mikhail S', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stalin, Joseph', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-05T19:24:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anastasia Edel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anastasia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Edel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/dad0e855-64d4-5db4-ab2e-39a216bd1a59', 'word_count': 1551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dad0e855-64d4-5db4-ab2e-39a216bd1a59'}, {'abstract': 'Much of life consists of musing moments when we look at something so beautiful or surprising that we wonder, briefly, who we are. This is the feeling the poet gave his readers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/05/books/john-ashbery-poet-appraisal.html', 'snippet': 'Much of life consists of musing moments when we look at something so beautiful or surprising that we wonder, briefly, who we are. This is the feeling the poet gave his readers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1965, when he was nearly 40 and his unorthodox talent was in full bloom, John Ashbery joined his friend Kenneth Koch for a mutual interview commissioned by a small press in Arizona. After a brief tongue-in-cheek discussion of how poems communicate, or don’t, Koch remarked that some uncertainties “enable the reader to escape from his ordinary consciousness of himself,” and suggested that it could be “very enjoyable” to feel like “a person you know nothing about.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/06/arts/06ashbery-appraisal/06ashbery-appraisal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/06/arts/06ashbery-appraisal/06ashbery-appraisal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/06/arts/06ashbery-appraisal/06ashbery-appraisal-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/06/arts/06ashbery-appraisal/06ashbery-appraisal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 975, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/06/arts/06ashbery-appraisal/06ashbery-appraisal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 975}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/06/arts/06ashbery-appraisal/06ashbery-appraisal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 631, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/06/arts/06ashbery-appraisal/06ashbery-appraisal-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1261, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline':