0e1-5563-beb8-cd07bf85620b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/0b797aa1-70e1-5563-beb8-cd07bf85620b'}, {'abstract': 'A photo-booth snapshot, a beloved set of cups, a Turkish rug: After a hurricane, Texans found ways to save valued possessions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/05/us/treasures-saved-harvey.html', 'snippet': 'A photo-booth snapshot, a beloved set of cups, a Turkish rug: After a hurricane, Texans found ways to save valued possessions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOUSTON — The floodwaters here ruined floors, walls, carpets, furniture, appliances and so much more. And yet the damage caused by Harvey was not total. The towers of debris cluttering the curbs of Houston are a deceptive measure of the storm’s devastation. Some things – small things, personal things – survived.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/06/us/06MOUNDS-saldana2/06MOUNDS-saldana2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/06/us/06MOUNDS-saldana2/06MOUNDS-saldana2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/06/us/06MOUNDS-saldana2/06MOUNDS-saldana2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/06/us/06MOUNDS-saldana2/06MOUNDS-saldana2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/06/us/06MOUNDS-saldana2/06MOUNDS-saldana2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 372}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/06/us/06MOUNDS-saldana2/06MOUNDS-saldana2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/06/us/06MOUNDS-saldana2/06MOUNDS-saldana2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1269, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What They Saved: Texans Reflect on Treasures Plucked From Harvey', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What They Saved: Treasures Plucked From a Hurricane', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Harvey (2017)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Houston (Tex)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-05T20:58:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manny Fernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58831378-50c7-59d8-a9c2-556c7206e418', 'word_count': 1095, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58831378-50c7-59d8-a9c2-556c7206e418'}, {'abstract': 'The architect Andrea Tognon lives and works on a property he got for free, which was once a neglected industrial complex.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/05/t-magazine/andrea-tognon-designer-architect-milan-home.html', 'snippet': 'The architect Andrea Tognon lives and works on a property he got for free, which was once a neglected industrial complex.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE NORTHERN MILAN district of Bicocca is not an obvious inspiration for an architect’s fantasies. Semi-industrial (but not in that noble wrought-iron way), perpetually bedraggled and largely devoid of historical significance, Bicocca is just 20 minutes from fashion’s golden triangle of Via Monte Napoleone, Via della Spiga and Via Sant’Andrea, but it’s as defiantly dour and dazzle-free as that area is refined and lacquered. The district’s signature feature is Borgo Pirelli, an early 20th-century village of red-roofed, low-slung apartment clusters, now used as public housing, built to keep the Pirelli tire factory workers tethered to the nearby assembly line. In 2004, the company turned one of its spaces into a cavernous gallery, HangarBicocca, featuring site-specific, 82-ton Anselm Kiefer installations, but there’s been no Bilbao effect, no open tap of artists streaming in searching for loft space. Change comes slowly in Italy, and the Milanese have never seen much charm in grittiness.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '152', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/t-magazine/tognon-slide-747D/tognon-slide-747D-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/05/t-magazine/tognon-slide-747D/tognon-slide-747D-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/t-magazine/tognon-slide-747D/tognon-slide-747D-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/05/t-magazine/tognon-slide-747D/tognon-slide-747D-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 440, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': '