elle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hamilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/21fdab17-6bd8-562e-a84d-77c489aceab0', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21fdab17-6bd8-562e-a84d-77c489aceab0'}, {'abstract': 'The number of C.I.A. deaths in Afghanistan rivals those killed in the Southeast Asia conflicts of nearly a half-century ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/06/world/asia/cia-afghanistan-war.html', 'snippet': 'The number of C.I.A. deaths in Afghanistan rivals those killed in the Southeast Asia conflicts of nearly a half-century ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — On a sweltering day earlier this summer, operatives with the Central Intelligence Agency gathered at Arlington National Cemetery to bury two of their own. Brian Ray Hoke and Nathaniel Patrick Delemarre, elite gunslingers who worked for the C.I.A.’s paramilitary force, were laid to rest after a firefight with Islamic State militants near Jalalabad in Afghanistan, close to the border with Pakistan.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/01/us/01dc-cia2/26dc-cia2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/01/us/01dc-cia2/26dc-cia2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/01/us/01dc-cia2/26dc-cia2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/01/us/01dc-cia2/26dc-cia2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/01/us/01dc-cia2/26dc-cia2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 614}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/01/us/01dc-cia2/26dc-cia2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1001, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/01/us/01dc-cia2/26dc-cia2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1814, 'width': 1773, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Funeral of 2 Friends: C.I.A. Deaths Rise in Secret Afghan War', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Funeral of 2 Friends: Secret War’s Toll on C.I.A.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Delemarre, Nathaniel Patrick (1969-2016)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoke, Brian Ray (1974-2016)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-06T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Goldman and Matthew Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6550eb91-2d9a-5094-aa7d-6ddf79f0c6ae', 'word_count': 1678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6550eb91-2d9a-5094-aa7d-6ddf79f0c6ae'}, {'abstract': 'First aid frames the world as an unpredictable set of chances to do your modest best.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/06/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-cpr-training.html', 'snippet': 'First aid frames the world as an unpredictable set of chances to do your modest best.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the beginning of summer, as I prepared to teach a seven-week writing workshop to 35 teenagers, I received a frightening email from my boss. Before class started, this email informed me, I had to pass a basic CPR-and-first-aid class. My first thought was, simply, Oh, no. Second thought: Maybe it’s not really required. (I Googled: It was.) Third thought — admittedly, more of a question, charged from within by a hot embarrassment that bordered on shame — What if I pass out?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10lor/10lor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10lor/10lor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10lor/10lor-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10lor/10lor-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10lor/10lor-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10lor/10lor-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10lor/10lor-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Letter of Recommendation: CPR Training', 'kicker': 'Letter of Recommendation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CPR Training', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-06T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter C. Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a9566d3-2dbd-54da-86a6-b7bcbb47d747', 'word_count': 899, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a9566d3-2dbd-54da-86a6-b7bcbb47d747'}, {'abstract': 'What one Alabama town’s attempt to secede from its school district tells us about the fragile progress of racial integration in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/06/magazine/the-resegregation-of-jefferson-county.html', 'snippet': 'What one Alabama town’s attempt to secede from its school district tells us about the fragile progress of racial integration in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2013, a flier began making the rounds in Gardendale, Ala., a suburb of Birmingham. On it, a blond white girl wearing a red backpack and knee-high socks peered innocently at a question hanging above her head: “Which path will Gardendale choose?” Beside her was a list of communities in Jefferson County — Pleasant Grove, Center Point/Huffman, Adamsville/Forestdale, Hueytown — under the heading: “Places that chose NOT to form their own school system.” Below that was a list of four communities — Homewood, Hoover, Vestavia Hills, Trussville — that did form their own school systems and were “listed as some of the best places to live in the country.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10alabama-1/10alabama-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10alabama-1/10alabama-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10alabama-1/10alabama-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10alabama-1/10alabama-1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10alabama-1/10alabama-1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10alabama-1/10alabama-1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 819, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10alabama-1/10alabama-1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1638, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Resegregation of Jefferson County', 'kicker': 'The Education Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dividing Lines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Segregation and Desegregation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jefferson County (Ala)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-06T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nikole Hannah-Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nikole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hannah-Jones', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae3ae5cc-fee0-5d77-9f7b-1a7c28bab85f', 'word_count': 9080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae3ae5cc-fee0-5d77-9f7b-1a7c28bab85f'}, {'abstract': 'Novels, graphic and otherwise, about the world at war, life with no future and imagined universes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/06/books/review/provenance-ann-leckie-best-fantasy-science-fiction.html', 'snippet': 'Novels, graphic and otherwise, about the world at war, life with no future and imagined universes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ann Leckie’s latest space romp, PROVENANCE (Orbit, $26), isn’t really a space opera in the same way as the award-winning Imperial Radch trilogy that made her famous. It might be tempting, therefore, to insist that comparisons are unfair. Yet they’re also unavoidable — largely because, even though “Provenance” centers on non-Radchaai societies, it is effectively a sequel. Now that a treaty has been established between the empire and the enigmatic, violent, technologically superior Presger, countless planets breathe easier. But the peace will last only so long as the “civilized” races of the universe actually act civilized.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/0910-BKS-Otherworldly/0910-BKS-Otherworldly-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/0910-BKS-Otherworldly/0910-BKS-Otherworldly-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/0910-BKS-Otherworldly/0910-BKS-Otherworldly-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/0910-BKS-Otherworldly/0910-BKS-Otherworldly-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/0910-BKS-Otherworldly/0910-BKS-Otherworldly-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/0910-BKS-Otherworldly/0910-BKS-Otherworldly-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', '