eported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73840426-c98d-57fa-85e3-e88f2afbb377', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73840426-c98d-57fa-85e3-e88f2afbb377'}, {'abstract': 'In its third year, this annual pop-up space for interactive installations attracts the likes of Jill Soloway, Emma Roberts and Jake Gyllenhaal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/06/arts/design/29rooms-is-a-creative-playhouse-for-the-instagram-set.html', 'snippet': 'In its third year, this annual pop-up space for interactive installations attracts the likes of Jill Soloway, Emma Roberts and Jake Gyllenhaal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The South Korean artist JeeYoung Lee stood surrounded by piles of New York City trash: 4,500 plastic water bottles, 1,500 wine corks, dozens of sheets of cardboard and recycled newspapers. Ms. Lee, who lives in Seoul, was in a warehouse in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, last week, turning the local garbage into a cheery seascape installation, complete with gulls and rolling waves against a backdrop the color of Caribbean waters. At the center of it was a sailboat, where visitors could perch, bobbing on a sea of their own detritus. By Ms. Lee’s request, the newspapers covering her boat were awash in articles about Hurricane Harvey.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/arts/07POP-SOCIAL/07POP-SOCIAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/07/arts/07POP-SOCIAL/07POP-SOCIAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/arts/07POP-SOCIAL/07POP-SOCIAL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType':