/t-magazine/bruce-chatwin-an-homage.html', 'snippet': 'The photographer Bruce Weber pays loving tribute to the British author and explorer, Bruce Chatwin, in these images of the actor Brenton Thwaites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The photographer Bruce Weber pays loving tribute to the British author and explorer, Bruce Chatwin, in these images of the actor Brenton Thwaites.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 479, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 479}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 818, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG/07tmag-chatwin-slide-XPFG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1636, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bruce Chatwin, an Homage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-09-07T12:52:09+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/736137c3-0daf-588c-87d4-3055739124a1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/736137c3-0daf-588c-87d4-3055739124a1'}, {'abstract': 'Forty years after the publication of his groundbreaking travelogue, ‘‘In Patagonia,’’ the author’s writing — and style — have lost none of their power to bewitch and inspire.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/07/t-magazine/bruce-chatwin.html', 'snippet': 'Forty years after the publication of his groundbreaking travelogue, ‘‘In Patagonia,’’ the author’s writing — and style — have lost none of their power to bewitch and inspire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1972, Bruce Chatwin left England and began to travel. This was not so long ago, and yet the world back then was still so unmapped: A traveler could and did wander through it like Pausanias, like Ibn Battuta, like the explorers who wrote the first drafts of the romance of movement, of the twinned danger and delight of finding oneself in a place where one was at the mercy of unfriendly but intriguing strangers. A traveler, back then, was far less likely to find in the foreign traces of the familiar. Depending on where he ventured, he had significantly fewer guarantees of safety, and equally thrillingly, only limited ways of communicating with those he knew and had left behind. And it wasn’t only that you could (and, indeed, would) travel as Ibn Battuta had, but, crucially, you could travel where Ibn Battuta had, as well: Sanaa and Baghdad and Damascus, all of which are now treacherous or off-\xadlimits, cities in countries forced by war or disaster or bad governance to deny their cultures’ extravagant senses of hospitality.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '128', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy/07tmag-chatwin-slide-B0MI-copy-superJum