ns', 'value': 'Tsipras, Alexis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-07T21:12:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Niki Kitsantonis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Niki', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kitsantonis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/963dd912-3488-5243-bb24-fddaefb3d18e', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/963dd912-3488-5243-bb24-fddaefb3d18e'}, {'abstract': 'Criminals gained access to certain files in the company’s system from mid-May to July, according to an investigation by Equifax.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/07/business/equifax-cyberattack.html', 'snippet': 'Criminals gained access to certain files in the company’s system from mid-May to July, according to an investigation by Equifax.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Equifax, one of the three major consumer credit reporting agencies, said on Thursday that hackers had gained access to company data that potentially compromised sensitive information for 143 million American consumers, including Social Security numbers and driver’s license numbers.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Equifax Says Cyberattack May Have Affected 143 Million in the U.S.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Equifax Attack Exposes Data Of 143 Million', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Identity Theft', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Equifax Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-07T21:16:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard, Tiffany Hsu, Nicole Perlroth and Ron Lieber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Tiffany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlroth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lieber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4dc22b2b-2945-5f98-afde-96be4ce045bd', 'word_count': 1275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4dc22b2b-2945-5f98-afde-96be4ce045bd'}, {'abstract': 'The rollback of the DACA program could disrupt many other aspects of immigrants’ lives, like health coverage, financial aid and driving privileges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/07/us/daca-losses-immigration.html', 'snippet': 'The rollback of the DACA program could disrupt many other aspects of immigrants’ lives, like health coverage, financial aid and driving privileges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is not just the threat of deportation that is hanging over Amparo Gonzalez. The 31-year-old single mother could also lose her job at a warehouse company, even if she stays in the country. With it would go the health insurance she gets through her employer, which covers her 13-year-old daughter, as well as the exams and treatments that Ms. Gonzalez needs for her chronic colon disease.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/us/08dreamers-01/08dreamers-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/07/us/08dreamers-01/08dreamers-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/us/08dreamers-01/08dreamers-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/us/08dreamers-01/08dreamers-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/07/us/08dreamers-01/08dreamers-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/us/08dreamers-01/08dreamers-01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/us/08dreamers-01/08dreamers-01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For DACA Recipients, Losing Protection and Work Permits Is Just the Start', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ripples Beyond Workplace For Most DACA Recipients', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-07T21:22:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caitlin Dickerson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caitlin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dickerson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a07b0ea-e1fb-5ae9-8be1-cae816359ff3', 'word_count': 1380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a07b0ea-e1fb-5ae9-8be1-cae816359ff3'}, {'abstract': 'On Thursday, Pierre Yovanovitch launches his own pieces for the home — in New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/07/t-magazine/design/pierre-yovanovitch-furniture.html', 'snippet': 'On Thursday, Pierre Yovanovitch launches his own pieces for the home — in New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The renowned French interior designer Pierre Yovanovitch has launched his debut furniture collection and opened his first public exhibition in the United States under the name “Oops,” of all monikers. He explains that word was his response when the R & Company gallery in TriBeCa, asked him to temporarily take over their space this fall (in French, “oops” often expresses surprise or being caught off guard). “The idea had been to create an installation using the furniture there and maybe some of my pieces,” Yovanovitch says. “But it became mostly my furniture — made especially for this. And, in the process, I realized you say ‘oops’ a lot of times during the day.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-pierre1/07tmag-pierre1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-pierre1/07tmag-pierre1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth