093610cea', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35fe79ef-50d2-5c29-b902-2cb093610cea'}, {'abstract': 'Revisiting Paul Scott’s “The Raj Quartet,” 70 years after the partition of India and Pakistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/08/books/review/raj-quartet-revisited.html', 'snippet': 'Revisiting Paul Scott’s “The Raj Quartet,” 70 years after the partition of India and Pakistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seventy years ago, a British government reeling from the costs of world war granted independence to India. The decision was made hastily and with a shocking lack of care for the people whose lives it affected and, in many cases, ended. The British partitioned the country at the moment they relinquished it, birthing Pakistan, and setting off population transfers and ethnic cleansing that left more than one million people dead. It was a fitting end: Far from pursuing a “civilizing mission,” the British Empire had exploited India, enforcing a policy of “divide and rule” and entrenching Hindu-Muslim tensions.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/10CriticsTakeChotiner1/10CriticsTakeChotiner1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/10CriticsTakeChotiner1/10CriticsTakeChotiner1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/10CriticsTakeChotiner1/10CriticsTakeChotiner1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/10CriticsTakeChotiner1/10CriticsTakeChotiner1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/10CriticsTakeChotiner1/10CriticsTakeChotiner1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 436}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/10CriticsTakeChotiner1/10CriticsTakeChotiner1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 744, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/books/review/10CriticsTakeChotiner1/10CriticsTakeChotiner1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1489, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Novels Explaining Britain’s Path From the Raj to Brexit', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Take', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Revisiting the Raj', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-08T09:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isaac Chotiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isaac', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chotiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7bbed54-2d06-5b24-a9bf-e4c7f723faa4', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7bbed54-2d06-5b24-a9bf-e4c7f723faa4'}, {'abstract': 'Business books can be treasure troves of unexpected similes and mind-boggling analogies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/08/magazine/new-sentences-from-21-secrets-of-million-dollar-sellers-by-stephen-j-harvill.html', 'snippet': 'Business books can be treasure troves of unexpected similes and mind-boggling analogies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Business books are Pez dispensers of breezy illustrative references: animal factoids, celebrity anecdotes, contextless quotation from famous writers, historical object lessons. They pop out, page by page, in a candy-colored stream that dissolves immediately on contact with the mind.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10mag-sentences1/10mag-sentences1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10mag-sentences1/10mag-sentences1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10mag-sentences1/10mag-sentences1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10mag-sentences1/10mag-sentences1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 496, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10mag-sentences1/10mag-sentences1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 496}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10mag-sentences1/10mag-sentences1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 847, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/magazine/10mag-sentences1/1