al cultural domination. Surprisingly, it is not an autobiography. The store of the title is an Amazon-like tech company with management anger issues, which reacts murderously when it discovers that a couple of married employees plan a tell-all book. If nothing else, it will leave you questioning the wisdom of drone delivery.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/17inside1/17inside1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 180, 'width': 180, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/17inside1/17inside1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 180, 'width': 180, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/17inside1/17inside1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/17inside1/17inside1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/17inside1/17inside1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'James Patterson’s Latest Villain Looks a Lot Like Amazon', 'kicker': 'Inside the List', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patterson, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Store (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tegmark, Max', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Life 3.0: Being Human in the Age of Artificial Intelligence (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-08T15:59:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/3863592a-7e4f-538d-9d10-1f7246e2e14d', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3863592a-7e4f-538d-9d10-1f7246e2e14d'}, {'abstract': 'In which we consult the Book Review’s past to shed light on the books of the present. This week: A look at Mikhail Gorbachev’s memoir and the former statesman’s divisive reputation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/08/books/review/bill-keller-mikhail-gorbachev-memoir.html', 'snippet': 'In which we consult the Book Review’s past to shed light on the books of the present. This week: A look at Mikhail Gorbachev’s memoir and the former statesman’s divisive reputation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s issue Peter Baker reviews a new biography about Mikhail Gorbachev, one of the 20th century’s most controversial figures. In 1996, Bill Keller reviewed Gorbachev’s memoir and tried to understand the former statesman’s divisive reputation. Below is an excerpt.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes From the Book Review Archives', 'kicker': 'Our Back Pages', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-08T16:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_d