, 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a5ec324-1750-5902-ac09-60d235915988', 'word_count': 1087, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a5ec324-1750-5902-ac09-60d235915988'}, {'abstract': 'Lindsey Graham and Patrick Leahy aim to replenish foreign aid and fortify the State Department.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/13/opinion/senators-lindsey-leahy-foreign-policy.html', 'snippet': 'Lindsey Graham and Patrick Leahy aim to replenish foreign aid and fortify the State Department.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The range of problems facing the United States abroad is daunting: a volatile Middle East, an unpredictable, mischievous Russia, a truly menacing North Korea. To say nothing of destabilizing global challenges like the mass migration of desperate refugees, and climate change. President Trump’s “America First” approach, which calls for disengagement from old alliances and responsibilities, is a dodge, not an answer. What’s needed is a robust foreign policy led by a reinvigorated State Department that right now is suffering from presidential neglect, poor leadership and an absence of professional firepower in pivotal positions.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/13/opinion/13wed1/13wed1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/13/opinion/13wed1/13wed1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/13/opinion/13wed1/13wed1-thum