btype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/17Leonhardt/17Leonhardt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/17Leonhardt/17Leonhardt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A History of Bloomberg’s Successes and Failures', 'kicker': 'Nonfiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mayor Mike and His Data', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Bloomberg: A Billionaire's Ambition (Book)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNickle, Christopher (Author)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mayors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-13T09:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/21711928-f9c8-5d9c-8db5-3d39a223975a', 'word_count': 1256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21711928-f9c8-5d9c-8db5-3d39a223975a'}, {'abstract': 'The past year has shown us that Americans have accepted the erasure of our history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/13/magazine/was-charlottesville-the-exception-or-the-rule.html', 'snippet': 'The past year has shown us that Americans have accepted the erasure of our history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1918 the Peruvian poet César Vallejo completed the collection of poems “Los Heraldos Negros,” known in English as “The Black Riders,” “The Black Messengers” or “The Black Heralds,” depending on the translator. “There are blows in life so powerful, I can’t answer” is a literal translation of the first line of the title poem. In the original Spanish, the line is “Hay golpes en la vida, tan fuertes. … Yo no sé.” As with the title, there has been some disagreement around how to translate “yo no sé.” “I don’t know,” “I can’t say” and “I can’t answer” are just three possibilities. Each translator has to confront Vallejo’s lacunas for the ungraspable experience of having to bear unbearable acts of power.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/12/magazine/12mag-whitesupremacy1/12mag-whitesupremacy1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/12/magazine/12mag-whitesupremacy1/12mag-whitesupremacy1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/12/magazine/12mag-whitesupremacy1/12mag-whitesupremacy1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/12/magazine/12mag-whitesupremacy1/12mag-whitesupremacy1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/12/magazine/12mag-whitesupremacy1/12mag-whitesupremacy1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/12/magazine/12mag-whitesupremacy1/12mag-whitesupremacy1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/12/magazine/12mag-whitesupremacy1/12mag-whitesupremacy1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Was Charlottesville the Exception or the Rule?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Charlottesville, Va, Violence (August, 2017)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-13T09:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia Rankine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rankine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c46c082-95ee-5bee-ac2c-7e279d6fe1ee', 'word_count': 1810, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c46c082-95ee-5bee-ac2c-7e279d6fe1ee'}, {'abstract': 'Two new crime novels travel back to the not-so-placid 1950s, while a third visits 19th-century Appalachia. Another tries to escape in a hot-air balloon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/13/books/review/thomas-mullen-lightning-men-new-crime-fiction.html', 'snippet': 'Two new crime novels travel back to the not-so-placid 1950s, while a third visits 19th-century Appalachia. Another tries to escape in a hot-air balloon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By 1950, some members of the Atlanta Ku Klux Klan think their raids have deteriorated into pathetic spectacles. “Jumping out of a parked car in a gravel lot hardly had the same grandeur as tearing across a field astride stallions, a fiery torch in one hand and a pistol in the other,” complains Dale Simpkins, an avid racist who participates in one such humiliating fiasco in Thomas Mullen’s LIGHTNING MEN (37Ink / Atria, $26). Lucius Boggs and Tommy Smith, two of the token black cops on the police force, are already run ragged trying to contain other crimes, like the violent turf wars between gangs fighting over the lucrative “reefer and shine” market. But with racial barriers slowly going down, officers are increasingly needed in middle-class neighborhoods like Hanford Park, where white residents are literally up in arms over the black families buying homes on their blocks.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/0917-BKS-Crime/0917-BKS-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/0917-BKS-Crime/0917-BKS-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/0917-BKS-Crime/0917-BKS-Crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/0917-BKS-Crime/0917-BKS-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/0917-BKS-Crime/0917-BKS-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 754}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/0917-BKS-Crime/0917-BKS-Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/books/review/0917-BKS-Crime/0917-BKS-Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1631, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bad Neighbors, Bad Husbands and Very Bad Behavior', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bad Neighbors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Lightning Men (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mullen, Thomas (1974- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Unquiet Grave (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCrumb, Sharyn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dead Woman Walking (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bolton, Sharon (1960- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Don't Let Go (Book)", 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coben, Harlan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-13T09:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f60b0f7-84d5-5e6d-964b-67f8a960b7dd', 'word_count': 862, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f60b0f7-84d5-5e6d-964b-67f8a960b7dd'}, {'abstract': 'How the Kremlin built one of the most powerful information weapons of the 21st century — and why it may be impossible to stop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/13/magazine/rt-sputnik-and-russias-new-theory-of-war.html', 'snippet': 'How the Kremlin built one of the most powerful information weapons of the 21st century — and why it may be impossible to stop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One morning in January 2016, Martin Steltner showed up at his office in the state courthouse building in western Berlin. Steltner, who has served for more than a dozen years as the spokesman for the Berlin state prosecutor, resembles a detective out of classic crime fiction: crisp suit, wavy gray hair and a gallows humor that comes with having seen it all. There was the 2009 case of the therapist who mistakenly killed two patients in an Ecstasy-infused session gone wrong. The Great Poker Heist of 2010, in which masked men stormed a celebrity-studded poker tournament with machetes and made off with a quarter-million dollars. The 2012 episode involving the Canadian porn star who killed and ate his boyfriend and then sent the leftovers home in the mail. Steltner embraced the oddball aspect of his job; he kept a picture of Elvis Presley on the wall of his office.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/magazine/17rt5/17rt5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/17/magazine/17rt5/17rt5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/magazine/17rt5/17rt5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit':