'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-14T11:02:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheryl Gay Stolberg and Yamiche Alcindor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheryl', 'middlename': 'Gay', 'lastname': 'Stolberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Yamiche', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alcindor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39c9521b-95ea-566f-a944-6e66bbb70fa0', 'word_count': 1450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39c9521b-95ea-566f-a944-6e66bbb70fa0'}, {'abstract': 'When will artists realize that they can’t thrive under authoritarianism?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/14/opinion/artist-putin-russia-serebrennikov.html', 'snippet': 'When will artists realize that they can’t thrive under authoritarianism?', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — At the beginning of August, five members of a gang accused of murdering 17 people during robberies on highways around the Moscow area were facing trial at Moscow Regional Court. While the five men were being escorted to the courtroom, they attacked their guards, seized their weapons and opened fire in the courthouse. Most likely this terrible episode could have been avoided had there been adequate security, but the five defendants were accompanied by only two armed guards.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/15/opinion/15kashin/15kashin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/15/opinion/15kashin/15kashin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption'