', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/14/t-magazine/vanseveren-slide-AHP6/vanseveren-slide-AHP6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 862, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/14/t-magazine/vanseveren-slide-AHP6/vanseveren-slide-AHP6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 862}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/14/t-magazine/vanseveren-slide-AHP6/vanseveren-slide-AHP6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 714, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/14/t-magazine/vanseveren-slide-AHP6/vanseveren-slide-AHP6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1427, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Architect David Van Severen, at Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-09-14T14:05:46+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/00fc20e3-8a1c-59ca-bee6-ae3d153a7f6a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/00fc20e3-8a1c-59ca-bee6-ae3d153a7f6a'}, {'abstract': 'In Brussels, the architect David Van Severen honored a building’s architectural history while renovating it for his family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/14/t-magazine/design/belgian-architect-david-van-severen-home.html', 'snippet': 'In Brussels, the architect David Van Severen honored a building’s architectural history while renovating it for his family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN EARLY 2016, the Belgian architect David Van Severen and his wife, the Swedish photographer Martina Bjorn, were looking for a larger apartment for themselves and their two young children, when Bjorn came across a curious property. ‘‘It was listed as having zero bedrooms,’’ she recalled recently. This was, it turned out, because the place was not technically an apartment; it had been, most recently, the offices of a law firm. But it was not technically office space, either — it was the bottom three floors of one of the opulent Beaux-Arts style mansions built by wealthy Bruxellois during the late 1800s, in the quiet, forested area south of the city center. Belgium’s architectural conservation laws had been notoriously lax, and so over the decades many of those buildings were demolished or carved up into offices or apartments. But the interiors of this one had somehow survived mostly intact, with ornate stucco moldings, marble fireplaces, wooden paneling, wrought-iron stair rails and parquet floors made in the traditional way, without a single nail. Huge windows on the grand top floor looked out across Avenue Winston Churchill on one side and into a densely wooded garden on the other.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '168', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/14/t-magazine/vanseveren-slide-YURB/vanseveren-slide-YURB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/14/t-magazine/vanseveren-slide-YURB/vanseveren-slide-YURB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/14/t-magazine/vanseveren-slide-YURB/vanseveren-slide-YURB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'i