b3c-57ad966814b2'}, {'abstract': 'After the “candlelight revolution,” the people push for a cleaner and fairer society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/14/opinion/south-korea-social-mobility.html', 'snippet': 'After the “candlelight revolution,” the people push for a cleaner and fairer society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the South Korean military killed hundreds of pro-democracy protesters and laid siege to the southwestern city of Gwangju in May 1980, few people outside the city knew what was happening. Martial law had been declared, the already censored press was completely gagged, and all communications with Gwangju were cut off. The end of the 10-day siege marked the beginning of a seven-year period of military repression and terror under Gen. Chun Doo-hwan, who outdid his predecessor, Gen. Park Chung-hee, in brutality and corruption.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/14/opinion/14WR-CHANG2-INYT/14WR-CHANG2-INYT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/14/opinion/14WR-CHANG2-INYT/14WR-CHANG2-INYT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/14/opinion/14WR-CHANG2-INYT/14WR-CHANG2-INYT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/14/opinion/14WR-CHANG2-INYT/14WR-CHANG2-INYT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 829, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/14/opinion/14WR-CHANG2-INYT/14WR-CHANG2-INYT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 829}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp