 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/12/insider/worldwar170913a/worldwar170913a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/12/insider/worldwar170913a/worldwar170913a-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/12/insider/worldwar170913a/worldwar170913a-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 564, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/12/insider/worldwar170913a/worldwar170913a-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1127, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Détente, Over a Cigarette', 'kicker': 'Picturing the First World War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Woodrow', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-14T21:55:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Insider', 'section_name': 'Times Insider', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30ea0a33-5dcd-5919-b9ce-9970b3b6777d', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30ea0a33-5dcd-5919-b9ce-9970b3b6777d'}, {'abstract': 'Douglas Brinkley on the writer whose fiction has captured the boom city’s relentless appetite for growth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/14/books/review/larry-mcmurtry-houston-trilogy-terms-of-endearment.html', 'snippet': 'Douglas Brinkley on the writer whose fiction has captured the boom city’s relentless appetite for growth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some claim the three essential books in Texas history are the Bible, the Warren Commission report and Larry McMurtry’s “Lonesome Dove,” his Pulitzer Prize-winning novel about 19th-century cattle drives. This last August, as I watched Hurricane Harvey intensify on my TV screen, the 50 inches of rain in Houston breaking national records, I stayed clear of the Book of Revelation and Oswald and instead hunkered down in safe-haven Austin reading McMurtry. Not, however, his western-themed “Lonesome Dove” — set in the Great Plains and Big Sky country — but his underappreciated Houston trilogy of “Moving On” (1970), “All My Friends Are Going to Be Strangers” (1972), and “Terms of Endearment” (1975), the best novels ever set in America’s fourth largest city. Additionally, his nonfiction essay collection, “In a Narrow Grave” (1968), remains the gold standard for understanding Houston’s brash rootlessness and civic insecurities.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/books/review/08Brinkleyessaysub/Brinkleyessaysub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/08/books/review/08Brinkleyessaysub/Brinkleyessaysub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/08/books/review/08Brinkleyessaysub/Brinkleyessaysub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 583, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop