igher education, a conversation among spies and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/15/books/review/letters-to-the-editor.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to the value of higher education, a conversation among spies and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters to the Editor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-15T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/c649fc7d-1ed1-5f39-82c8-c6f464445350', 'word_count': 459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c649fc7d-1ed1-5f39-82c8-c6f464445350'}, {'abstract': 'In which we consult the Book Review’s past to shed light on the books of the present. This week: Salman Rushdie on importance of “global dialogue.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/15/books/review/salman-rushdie-global-dialogue.html', 'snippet': 'In which we consult the Book Review’s past to shed light on the books of the present. This week: Salman Rushdie on importance of “global dialogue.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week features a review of Salman Rushdie’s new novel, “The Golden House.” In 2005, Rushdie, then president of PEN America, wrote a powerful essay stressing the importance of “global dialogue” when the world seemed the most isolated. Below is an excerpt:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes From the Book Review Archives', 'kicker': 'Our Back Pages', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-15T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d03a2eee-c9b9-5109-9bc2-5042cfe1b7bc', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d03a2eee-c9b9-5109-9bc2-5042cfe1b7bc'}, {'abstract': 'Keep area clear of bystanders. In rare cases, use Sawzall or arc welder to sever the figure’s feet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/15/magazine/how-to-pull-down-a-statue.html', 'snippet': 'Keep area clear of bystanders. In rare cases, use Sawzall or arc welder to sever the figure’s feet.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It is quite easy” to topple a statue, says Hanna Bondar, the former deputy chief architect for the city of Kiev. Don’t be intimidated by what seem to be unbreakable materials — stone, concrete, bronze can all be brought down. By 1991, when the Soviet state collapsed, some 5,500 statues of the Communist leader Vladimir Lenin had been erected in Ukraine, the highest density anywhere in the U.S.S.R. In December 2013, a mob of anti-Kremlin protesters knocked over a hulking red quartzite Lenin on one of Kiev’s main squares, setting off a wave of removals that came to be known as “Leninfall.” At its peak, in February 2014, 376 Lenins were felled in a single month.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/magazine/17tip/17tip-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/17/magazine/17tip/17tip-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 't