e', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/tmag-intheair-slide-1NK4/tmag-intheair-slide-1NK4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/tmag-intheair-slide-1NK4/tmag-intheair-slide-1NK4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 524, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/tmag-intheair-slide-1NK4/tmag-intheair-slide-1NK4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 524}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/tmag-intheair-slide-1NK4/tmag-intheair-slide-1NK4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 894, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/tmag-intheair-slide-1NK4/tmag-intheair-slide-1NK4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1789, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Timeless, Imperfect Beauty of the Handmade', 'kicker': 'In the Air', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quilts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-15T13:51:24+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/3b3ad948-7c5e-534f-a283-ecae606e28d6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/3b3ad948-7c5e-534f-a283-ecae606e28d6'}, {'abstract': 'Every body of work comes to an end. And judging by recent late-career achievements, the best is sometimes saved for last.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/15/t-magazine/art/artists-final-last-works.html', 'snippet': 'Every body of work comes to an end. And judging by recent late-career achievements, the best is sometimes saved for last.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE OF THE CURSES of the modern age is the terminal diagnosis. We live each day knowing that we will die, but most of us find it easy enough, and more than congenial, to forget about it. To be told that you have two years or one year or six months left is to be excused from a paradise of ignorance into rude knowledge. And yet to have this appointment in Samarra inked on your schedule is also a kind of blessing or special power. We expect that those facing death ought to live a little differently because of it. They have an opportunity, or so we think — to put things right, to make reparations and bequests and ‘‘arrangements.’’ To book a special experience, long desired but deferred. To prepare a statement. For artists, the knowledge that the end is beckoning may provoke a last surge of inspiration to begin or complete final works, works that invariably come to acquire an extra significance, no matter how flawed the works themselves.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '150', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/15tmag-legacies/15tmag-legacies-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/15tmag-legacies/15tmag-legacies-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/15tmag-legacies/15tmag-legacies-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/15tmag-legacies/15tmag-legacies-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 440, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/15tmag-legacies/15tmag-legacies-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 440}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/15tmag-legacies/15tmag-legacies-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/15/t-magazine/15tmag-legacies/15tmag-legacies-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1502, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Do Artists’ Final Works Say About Their Lives?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Curtain Falls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AKERMAN, CHANTAL', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Leonard', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bowie, David', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-15T13:56:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Art', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Smallwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smallwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/390dbcbd-7d08-5680-a92a-46023ac1f05b', 'word_count': 1498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/390dbcbd-7d08-5680-a92a-46023ac1f05b'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. McBride, a star and writer of HBO’s “Vice Principals,” talks about his foulmouthed characters, moving to the South (again) and remaking a horror classic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/15/arts/television/danny-mcbride-vice-principals.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. McBride, a star and writer of HBO’s “Vice Principals,” talks about his foulmouthed characters, moving to the South (again) and remaking a horror classic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By all accounts, Danny McBride is not that guy — not Kenny Powers, the boorish former Major League Baseball pitcher turned substitute phys ed teacher he played for four seasons in “Eastbound & Down.” Not Neal Gamby, the nasty-tempered, aggressively striving school administrator he now plays in “Vice Principals.” Not a redneck. Not even a sitcom fan.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/arts/17SNAPSHOT/17SNAPSHOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/17/arts/17SNAPSHOT/17SNAPSHOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/arts/17SNAPSHOT/17SNAPSHOT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/arts/17SNAPSHOT/17SNAPSHOT-articleLarge.jpg',