28, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/opinion/sunday/10shermanCOVER/10shermanCOVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/10/opinion/sunday/10shermanCOVER/10shermanCOVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/10/opinion/sunday/10shermanCOVER/10shermanCOVER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'They Have Money. (Shhh.)', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They Have Money. (Shhh.)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-16T18:31:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/0786a5b2-59f6-5ef2-a9d6-ffcf67301989', 'word_count': 1495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0786a5b2-59f6-5ef2-a9d6-ffcf67301989'}, {'abstract': 'The Rev. Joseph De Laine, an unsung activist, deserves to be honored.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/16/opinion/sunday/statue-south-carolina-de-laine.html', 'snippet': 'The Rev. Joseph De Laine, an unsung activist, deserves to be honored.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you look closely at Brown v. Board of Education, the landmark 1954 school desegregation decision, you’ll see that Brown wasn’t a single case. It was five cases consolidated into one. Briggs v. Elliott, the first of them, took place in my home state, South Carolina. Briggs came about after the Rev. Joseph De Laine organized a group of black parents in Clarendon County to petition for equal educational facilities. The man who stood fast against that request — the Elliott of Briggs v. Elliott — was the chairman of the school board, Roderick Miles Elliott. Or as he was known in my family, Uncle Roddy.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/opinion/sunday/17elliott1/17elliott1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/17/opinion/sunday/17elliott1/17elliott1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/opinion/sunday/17elliott1/17elliott1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/opinion/sunday/17elliott1/17delaine1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 748, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/17/opinion/sunday/17elliott1/17delaine1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 748}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/opinion/sunday/17elliott1/17delaine1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 822, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/17/opinion/sunday/17elliott1/17delaine1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1643, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Erect a Statue of This Civil Rights Hero', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Statue We Need', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights Movement (1954-68)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brown v Board of Education (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-16T18:51:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a851d6b-aa4b-576e-b04b-569154070f83', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a851d6b-aa4b-576e-b04b-569154070f83'}, {'abstract': 'Extreme fire behavior — difficult to predict and dangerous to fight — has been the watchword for the year. And these fires are menacing more lives and property.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/16/us/wildfires-western-states.html', 'snippet': 'Extreme fire behavior — difficult to predict and dangerous to fight — has been the watchword for the year. And these fires are menacing more lives and property.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TROUTDALE, Ore. — Some fires suddenly exploded in size. One in Montana doubled in 24 hours, charring 78 square miles overnight — an area bigger than Brooklyn. Already burning fires started new ones, shooting embers like artillery barrages, including one that apparently jumped several miles across the Columbia River into Washington from Oregon, breaching a natural firebreak that long seemed impregnable.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': No