nippet': 'Set in a real home, an unnamed woman cooks while she relates piercing tales about the horrors in Syria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Halfway through “Oh My Sweet Land,” the writer-director Amir Nizar Zuabi’s wrenching and shrewd solo show, you will begin to worry about the onions. You have heard them sizzle in the pan, seen the steam rise up, smelled the sweet-savory aroma. And then you should have forgotten those onions, because the unnamed woman frying them (a ferocious and radiant Nadine Malouf) is telling you a terrible story. The story is about a prisoner who knows he must leave Syria when a captain of the secret police brings the man’s daughter to the jail. The captain tickles her and plies her with chocolates, an oblique and sickening threat.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/20/arts/20SWEETLAND/18SWEETLAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/20/arts/20SWEETLAND/18SWEETLAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/20/arts/20SWEETLAND/18SWEETLAND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/20/arts/20SWEETLAND/18SWEETLAND-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/20/arts/20SWEETLAND/18SWEETLAND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/20/arts/20SWEETLAND/18SWEETLAND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/20/arts/20SWEETLAND/18SWEETLAND-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Oh My Sweet Land,’ Dinner Is Served. Don’t Come Hungry.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love, War and Sizzling Meat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuabi, Amir Nizar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Play Co, The (Theater Co)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malouf, Nadine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Oh My Sweet Land (Play)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-18T02:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d0de86c-b32b-57e2-b189-eb7875660892', 'word_count': 1001, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d0de86c-b32b-57e2-b189-eb7875660892'}, {'abstract': 'The play, about a single mother with five children, explores the dimensions of American sexual and societal guilt in a first-rate revival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/17/theater/review-in-the-blood-suzan-lori-parks.html', 'snippet': 'The play, about a single mother with five children, explores the dimensions of American sexual and societal guilt in a first-rate revival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tragedy stalks Hester La Negrita, the heroine of Suzan-Lori Parks’s “In the Blood,” as relentlessly as it does the doomed queens of Euripides and Racine. Played with exquisitely clouded radiance by Saycon Sengbloh in the Signature Theater’s first-rate revival of this genre-mutating 1999 drama, the illiterate Hester would probably never presume to talk in such highfalutin terms.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/arts/18INTHEBLOOD2/18INTHEBLOOD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 423, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/18/arts/18INTHEBLOOD2/18INTHEBLOOD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 423}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/arts/18INTHEBLOOD2/18INTHEBLOOD2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/arts/18INTHEBLOOD2/18INTHEBLOOD2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1444, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/arts/18INTHEBLOOD2/18INTHEBLOOD2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/18/arts/18INTHEBLOOD2/18INTHEBLOOD2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/arts/18INTHEBLOOD2/18INTHEBLOOD2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘In the Blood’ and the Singular Talent of Suzan-Lori Parks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'She’s Doomed, but No One Is Blameless', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Signature Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Signature Theater Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benson, Sarah (Theater Director)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parks, Suzan-Lori', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sengbloh, Saycon', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'In the Blood (Play)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-18T02:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccd86466-0b16-56ba-9e57-f5113f03a053', 'word_count': 1127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccd86466-0b16-56ba-9e57-f5113f03a053'}, {'abstract': 'Vinnie and Eileen flash some entrepreneurial spirit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/17/arts/television/the-deuce-recap-show-and-prove.html', 'snippet': 'Vinnie and Eileen flash some entrepreneurial spirit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The second episode of “The Deuce” is book ended by scenes of two cops, Alston and Flanagan, rounding up prostitutes to toss in the paddy wagon. As law enforcement, this catch-and-release ritual seems to accomplish little more than irritating the pimps, who have surely budgeted for it anyway. The cops face annoyance but not resistance: The women dutifully pile into the van and sit around in lockup, waiting to get bailed out. In a particularly satisfying detail, they’re brought in on