_count': 762, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c50bbe5-5b4e-5dfd-9723-285ce68ed05b'}, {'abstract': 'But practical-minded utility executives are still shutting down coal-fired plants in favor of more affordable energy sources.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/18/opinion/using-the-epa-to-prop-up-big-coal.html', 'snippet': 'But practical-minded utility executives are still shutting down coal-fired plants in favor of more affordable energy sources.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Trump administration is unflinching in its misbegotten campaign to protect the coal industry from what has become an obvious and inevitable decline. Eight months in, the administration has already killed, or is in the process of killing, rules that would prevent the dumping of coal mining wastes in streams, impose a temporary moratorium on new mine leases in the West, and reduce greenhouse gas emissions from coal-fired power plants — one of President Barack Obama’s most important efforts to resist climate change. All of this to prop up an industry whose workers would be best served not by false promises of new mining jobs, but by aggressive programs to retrain them for a changing economy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon1web/18mon1web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon1web/18mon1web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon1web/18mon1web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon1web/18mon1web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon1web/18mon1web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon1web/18mon1web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon1web/18mon1web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1420, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Using the E.P.A. to Prop Up Big Coal', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Using the E.P.A. to Prop Up Big Coal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pruitt, Scott', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-18T07:21:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/01eb6385-0d73-5fbb-a8de-95eb1c1177e5', 'word_count': 662, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01eb6385-0d73-5fbb-a8de-95eb1c1177e5'}, {'abstract': 'The Party authorities let us scream obscenities and insult the system. But did we change society?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/18/opinion/punk-rock-communist-yugoslavia.html', 'snippet': 'The Party authorities let us scream obscenities and insult the system. But did we change society?', 'lead_paragraph': 'ZAGREB, Croatia — In Sarajevo, Yugoslavia, during the 1984 Winter Olympics, Elvis J. Kurtovic and his Meteors were performing a cover of Bob Dylan’s “Maggie’s Farm” in a stifling little basement hall before 300 wild punk rock fans. But it wasn’t Dylan’s rendition they were playing, it was their own, a deliberate extension that ostensibly was about Margaret Thatcher, the prime minister of Britain. But actually, Elvis J. Kurtovic and his Meteors were singing about — in an extremely brutal way that should have been unacceptable to the Communist authorities — Yugoslavia’s own prime minister, Milka Planinc.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18jergovic3web/18jergovic3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18jergovic3web/18jergovic3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18jergovic3web/18jergovic3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18jergovic3web/18jergovic3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18jergovic3web/18jergovic3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 417}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18jergovic3web/18jergovic3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 711, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18jergovic3web/18jergovic3web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1422, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Punk Rock Meant to Communist Yugoslavia', 'kicker': 'Red Century', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pop and Rock Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tito, Josip Broz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yugoslavia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Eastern Europe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-18T07:21:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miljenko Jergovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miljenko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jergovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ca5b150-b41f-5e9f-8a93-cbeea3943de0', 'word_count': 1283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ca5b150-b41f-5e9f-8a93-cbeea3943de0'}, {'abstract': 'The U.N. is unlikely to pass new sanctions, but there is nothing to stop Washington from acting on its own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/18/opinion/rohingya-myanmar-military-sanctions.html', 'snippet': 'The U.N. is unlikely to pass new sanctions, but there is nothing to stop Washington from acting on its own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A year ago, when President Barack Obama announced that, given Myanmar’s progress on democracy, the United States would lift remaining sanctions on Myanmar’s military, rights groups warned that easing the pressure was premature: The country’s democratic transition was incomplete, they said, the military retained vast powers and its record on human rights was dismal.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon2web/18mon2web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon2web/18mon2web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon2web/18mon2web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon2web/18mon2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon2web/18mon2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon2web/18mon2web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/18/opinion/18mon2web/18mon2web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Squeeze Myanmar’s Military', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Squeeze Myanmar’s Military', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 's