me to New York to see the Empire State Building, the Statue of Liberty and Fiorucci.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/19/t-magazine/fiorucci-new-york-store-history.html', 'snippet': '“You come to New York to see the Empire State Building, the Statue of Liberty and Fiorucci.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1976, a bustling retail store opened on East 59th Street in New York: It was called Fiorucci — a global concept shop by the Italian designer Elio Fiorucci, who had opened other outposts in London and Milan. It housed Fiorucci’s own fashion line, which had become famous for baby-faced angels on T-shirts and some of the first stretch jeans, as well as pieces from other designers, beauty products and accessories.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/style/19tmag-fiorucci-slide-ME57/19tmag-fiorucci-slide-ME57-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/24/style/19tmag-fiorucci-slide-ME57/19tmag-fiorucci-slide-ME57-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/style/19tmag-fiorucci-slide-ME57/19tmag-fiorucci-slide-ME57-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/style/19tmag-fiorucci-slide-ME57/19tmag-fiorucci-slide-ME57-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/24/style/19tmag-fiorucci-slide-ME57/19tmag-fiorucci-slide-ME57-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 