'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/21/t-magazine/gardens-slide-1JIX/gardens-slide-1JIX-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1537, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Art in the Wild', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-09-21T14:20:15+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/961a4692-75b8-5ce5-9b3f-822d5eba07ec', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/961a4692-75b8-5ce5-9b3f-822d5eba07ec'}, {'abstract': 'Why three European art parks — outdoor spaces with large-scale, site-specific sculpture — have become essential places to engage with culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/21/t-magazine/design/european-art-parks-sculpture.html', 'snippet': 'Why three European art parks — outdoor spaces with large-scale, site-specific sculpture — have become essential places to engage with culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 2002, Jenny Holzer was invited to create a piece at Wanas Konst, an 80-acre sculpture park tucked behind a romantic 16th-century step-gabled castle in the wilds of southern Sweden. She spent two days just wandering the grounds, where contemporary sculptures (a monumental steel chair covered in antlers, a tiny wood house in a clearing) by the likes of Marina Abramovic, Robert Wilson and Maya Lin stand amid tall beech trees and, in some cases, grazing cows. (The art foundation shares the land with a working dairy farm.) Deep in the woods, Holzer found herself thinking of ‘‘fairy tales light and dark.’’ Then she came upon a waist-high stone wall that dates to the 1700s. ‘‘I started following it and knew it was the site,’’ she says. Her contribution 