ce': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/20/opinion/20rosenthalWeb/20rosenthalWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/20/opinion/20rosenthalWeb/20rosenthalWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/20/opinion/20rosenthalWeb/20rosenthalWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 633, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/20/opinion/20rosenthalWeb/20rosenthalWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1266, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/20/opinion/20rosenthalWeb/20rosenthalWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/20/opinion/20rosenthalWeb/20rosenthalWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/20/opinion/20rosenthalWeb/20rosenthalWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'So, a President Walks Up to a Microphone...', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-21T14:50:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/840e5488-22d2-5ba4-aeb5-2c1627221fe2', 'word_count': 713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/840e5488-22d2-5ba4-aeb5-2c1627221fe2'}, {'abstract': 'Authenticity in travel has become an increasingly implausible endeavor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/21/magazine/going-local-is-it-possible-as-a-foreigner-to-experience-a-country-as-its-people-do.html', 'snippet': 'Authenticity in travel has become an increasingly implausible endeavor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the last few decades, a curious plague came to afflict Turkey: British luxury-travel websites or, as they were known in the tourism industry, “specialist tour operators.” The most notorious was Exclusive Escapes (a.k.a. Hidden Turkey). According to one advertising executive’s blog, its distinction was to offer “off-the-beaten-track Turkish holidays for the discerning middle classes” — some hotel rooms were $300 a night, but “class” is a moving target — and for those Britons who, the executive imagined, “wanted to feel they were getting away from the crowds,” who craved “an authentic experience.” Glorious boutique hotels sprang up along the shores of the Mediterranean and the Aegean, their multiterraced buildings cascading down Turkey’s mountain slopes to the water’s edge. The website promised rooms that had windows big enough to let in entire seas and real Turkish breakfasts while you watched the graceful gulet boats drift by. It’s Turkey. Turkey is magical. It ought to be hard to get a vacation in Turkey wrong.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/magazine/24mag-24opener1/24mag-24opener_intro-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/24/magazine/24mag-24opener1/24mag-24opener_intro-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/magazine/24mag-24opener1/24mag-24opener_intro-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/magazine/24mag-24opener1/24mag-24opener_intro-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/magazine/24mag-24opener1/24mag-24opener_intro-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/24/magazine/24mag-24opener1/24mag-24opener_intro-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/magazine/24mag-24opener1/24mag-24opener_intro-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Going Local: Is It Possible, as a Foreigner, to Experience a Country as Its People Do?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Going Local', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Istanbul (Turkey)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-21T14:52:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suzy Hansen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suzy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hansen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c955e303-0a53-5065-b224-0f04f46f47b6', 'word_count': 2187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c955e303-0a53-5065-b224-0f04f46f47b6'}, {'abstract': 'Some steps brides can take on their own to prepare and nurture their skin in the weeks, days and hours before their wedding.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/21/fashion/weddings/a-brides-countdown-to-glowing-skin.html', 'snippet': 'Some steps brides can take on their own to prepare and nurture their skin in the weeks, days and hours before their wedding.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brides are supposed to glow on their wedding day, and that, of course, includes their skin. But who has the time to keep running to the dermatologist’s office for a peel, facial or fillers, especially with so many other chores to tend to?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/fashion/weddings/24SKINCARE-BRIDE-COMBO1/24SKINCARE-BRIDE-COMBO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/24/fashion/weddings/24SKINCARE-BRIDE-COMBO1/24SKINCARE-BRIDE-COMBO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/fashion/weddings/24SKIN