09-22T11:50:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sewell Chan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sewell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/328f02a8-7398-5617-85c3-5e94a6786e87', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/328f02a8-7398-5617-85c3-5e94a6786e87'}, {'abstract': 'The Quad Cinema salutes the actor, who died this month, with a retrospective.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/22/movies/harry-dean-stanton-retrospective-quad-cinema.html', 'snippet': 'The Quad Cinema salutes the actor, who died this month, with a retrospective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With “Lucky,” opening Friday, Sept. 29, Harry Dean Stanton, who died this month, landed the role of a lifetime as a 90-year-old, desert-town atheist seeking spiritual enlightenment as he confronts the void beyond. (This directorial debut from John Carroll Lynch was written by Logan Sparks and Drago Sumonja with Mr. Stanton in mind.) In “Also Starring Harry Dean Stanton,” Sept. 22-Oct. 1, the Quad Cinema in Manhattan rolls out 21 movies that propelled the laconic character actor and his soulfully furrowed countenance to cult stardom. The lineup includes B. L. Norton’s “Cisco Pike,” Monte Hellman’s “Cockfighter,” Ridley Scott’s “Alien” and Howard Deutch’s “Pretty in Pink,” as well as Mr. Stanton’s first real lead, in Alex Cox’s “Repo Man” (1984), and his breakthrough months later as a mostly silent drifter yearning to reconnect with his estranged family in Wim Wenders’s “Paris, Texas.” Mr. Stanton won the part after he confessed to its writer Sam Shepard, who died in July, in a Santa Fe bar that he longed “to play something of some beauty or sensitivity.” (quadcinema.com)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE3/24GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE3/24GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE3/24GUIDE3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE3/24GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE3/24GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE3/24GUIDE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE3/24GUIDE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Harry Dean Stanton, and the Movies That Broke Him Free', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Film; Paying Tribute To a Quiet Star', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Quad Cinema', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stanton, Harry Dean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-22T12:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1de91a13-e9b8-5272-a2d2-d2d28f6cad45', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1de91a13-e9b8-5272-a2d2-d2d28f6cad45'}, {'abstract': 'Joanne Froggatt (“Downton Abbey”) and Ioan Gruffudd (“Fantastic Four”) play a couple whose date leads to a vicious she-said, he-said war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/22/arts/television/joanne-froggatt-liar-sundancetv.html', 'snippet': 'Joanne Froggatt (“Downton Abbey”) and Ioan Gruffudd (“Fantastic Four”) play a couple whose date leads to a vicious she-said, he-said war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joanne Froggatt, who twisted hearts as Anna Bates, the put-upon lady’s maid in “Downton Abbey,” subjects herself to yet another round of hardship in “Liar,” starting Wednesday, Sept. 27, on SundanceTV. Written by the brothers Harry and Jack Williams (“The Missing”), this six-part psychological thriller, which debuted in Britain this month, stars Ms. Froggatt as Laura Nielson, a teacher recently split from her long-term boyfriend, and Ioan Gruffudd (“Fantastic Four”) as Andrew Earlham, a widowed surgeon and father of one of her students, who asks her out on a date.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE6/24GUIDE6-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE6/24GUIDE6-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE6/24GUIDE6-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE6/24GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE6/24GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE6/24GUIDE6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE6/24GUIDE6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1381, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Did That Date Go? In ‘Liar,’ It Depends on Who’s Telling', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Television; ‘Rashomon’ Goes On a Bad Date', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Liar (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SundanceTV', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Froggatt, Joanne', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-22T12:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25430d73-00b7-5ad3-b575-806dd4a23c79', 'word_count': 155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25430d73-00b7-5ad3-b575-806dd4a23c79'}, {'abstract': 'The trumpeter and composer organized “For/With,” a two-night event at Issue Project Room that focuses on four composers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/22/arts/music/nate-wooley-issue-project-room.html', 'snippet': 'The trumpeter and composer organized “For/With,” a two-night event at Issue Project Room that focuses on four composers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The trumpeter and composer Nate Wooley favors subtlety over bombast and slowly winding structures over obvious drama. His enigmatic sound world can seem to echo the unpredictability of the rest of the world. An artist of rare integrity and artistic ambition, he is also a keen collaborator, and has organized “For/With,” a two-night mini-festival at Issue Project Room in Brooklyn that focuses on four composers — Christian Wolff, Ashley Fure, Michael Pisaro and Annea Lockwood — from whom he has commissioned works. “For,” on Friday, Sept. 29, includes Mr. Wolff’s solo “For Trumpet Player” and Ms. Lockwood’s tribute to Pauline Oliveros alongside Ms. Fure’s percussion solo “Shiver Lung 2” and an improvised duo for Mr. Wolff (on percussion) and Mr. Pisaro (electric guitar). “With,” on Saturday, Sept. 30, features Mr. Pisaro’s piece for Mr. Wooley, “Stem-Flower-Root,” works by Ms. Lockwood and Mr. Wolff, and a discussion with the composers. (8 p.m., Downtown Brooklyn, issueprojectroom.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE7/24GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE7/24GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 733}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE7/24GUIDE7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 838, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE7/24GUIDE7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1797, 'width': 1470, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE7/24GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE7/24GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE7/24GUIDE7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nate Wooley Puts Together a Mini-Festival', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classical; A Collaborator’s Commissions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Issue Project Room', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wooley, Nate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oliveros, Pauline', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-22T12:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7476625a-b796-55b6-9be8-f891208efa04', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7476625a-b796-55b6-9be8-f891208efa04'}, {'abstract': 'As part of the art extravaganza “Pacific Standard Time: LA/LA,” an exhibition focuses on Walt Disney’s trip to South America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/22/arts/design/walt-disney-pacific-standard-time-la-la.html', 'snippet': 'As part of the art extravaganza “Pacific Standard Time: LA/LA,” an exhibition focuses on Walt Disney’s trip to South America.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Pacific Standard Time: LA/LA,” the art extravaganza that fills some 70 Southern California art institutions to celebrate the foundational presence of Latin American art in the region, is now under full sail. And probably none of its exhibitions more accessibly capture the hemispheric dynamic than “How to Read El Pato Pascual: Disney’s Latin America and Latin America’s Disney.” In 1941, Walt Disney traveled to South America with a small army of artists and screenwriters as part of the United States government’s postwar “Good Neighbor” policy. The trip produced Latinized versions of Donald Duck and Mickey Mouse in Disney films and accusations of cultural imperialism by Latin American artists. The tale unfolds in this ingeniously thought-out Los Angeles show (through Jan. 14) divided between the MAK Center for Art and Architecture at the Schindler House and the Luckman Fine Arts Complex at the California State University, Los Angeles.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE4/24GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 316, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/arts/24GUIDE4/24GUIDE4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 316, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name