n_name': "Women's Runway", 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/0c04dcbf-42a3-5ea9-b1b3-ee9ddd5c899b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/0c04dcbf-42a3-5ea9-b1b3-ee9ddd5c899b'}, {'abstract': 'Working from a man’s perspective allowed me to wriggle free of certain expectations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/22/opinion/sunday/i-knew-i-had-to-fight-for-authority-so-i-wrote-like-a-man.html', 'snippet': 'Working from a man’s perspective allowed me to wriggle free of certain expectations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On forms to be filled out in waiting rooms, I always hesitate over the question of occupation: writer or author? For years it was only writer; now it’s a question of mood. Writer forever has her work ahead of her. Author has already done it. Writer bears no great claim: Like anyone else, she is just scrabbling away at it, unsure, experimenting. Author comes with distinction, and the right to expect that she will be read. Now, though, I think the perceived honor of the word is wrapped up in “author” sounding like a chip off the granite block of authority. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24krauss/24krauss-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24krauss/24krauss-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24krauss/24krauss-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u