to do better and have promised to be more transparent about who is paying for political ads. That’s a good start, but more is required — of Facebook, of social media giants generally and of Congress.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23sat1/-23sat1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23sat1/-23sat1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23sat1/-23sat1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23sat1/-23sat1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23sat1/23sat1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23sat1/23sat1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23sat1/23sat1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Facebook’s Belated Awakening', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Facebook’s Belated Awakening', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Interference in 2016 US Elections and Ties to Trump Associates', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-23T00:18:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f22252b-d419-52e9-a2ab-c91c51a07898', 'word_count': 661, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f22252b-d419-52e9-a2ab-c91c51a07898'}, {'abstract': 'Making payments while sick is overwhelming. A piece of bipartisan legislation could change that.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/22/opinion/cancer-patients-student-loans-deferment.html', 'snippet': 'Making payments while sick is overwhelming. A piece of bipartisan legislation could change that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Education Secretary Betsy DeVos has recently drawn criticism for proposing a long list of cuts to higher education programs. Congress must mull those potentially traumatic trims while reauthorizing standard financing for students and colleges, but there’s one positive change that might sneak by in the process: the bipartisan Deferment for Active Cancer Treatment Act, which would let borrowers defer their student loan payments when they get a cancer diagnosis — and for six more months after their active treatment is completed. It’s a courtesy Congress extends now to borrowers who are unemployed, deployed in the military or volunteering with the Peace Corps.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/22/opinion/22ritzelWeb/22ritzelWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/22/opinion/22ritzelWeb/22ritzelWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/22/opinion/22ritzelWeb/22ritzelWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/22/opinion/22ritzelWeb/22ritzelWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/22/opinion/22ritzelWeb/22ritzelWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/22/opinion/22ritzelWeb/22ritzelWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/22/opinion/22ritzelWeb/22ritzelWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Give Cancer Patients a Break on Student Loans', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let Cancer Patients Defer Student Loans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-23T00:20:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca J. Ritzel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Ritzel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb836c66-675a-5a5c-b0bd-a577e1c09614', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb836c66-675a-5a5c-b0bd-a577e1c09614'}, {'abstract': 'By the fall of 1967, Lyndon Johnson had finally realized that he could not fund guns and butter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/22/opinion/vietnam-war-great-society.html', 'snippet': 'By the fall of 1967, Lyndon Johnson had finally realized that he could not fund guns and butter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Lyndon Johnson surely felt a bitter sense of recognition when he opened The Washington Post on Aug. 1, 1967. There, on Page A12, appeared a political cartoon — the latest by the brilliant cartoonist Herbert Block, better known as Herblock. The sketch showed a beleaguered Johnson flanked by two female suitors. To his right stood a voluptuous seductress bedecked with jewels and a mink stole bearing the words “Vietnam War.” To his left was a scrawny, disheveled waif labeled “U.S. Urban Needs.” The Johnson figure reassured them, “There’s money enough to support both of you,” but readers could hardly fail to grasp the president’s hesitation. The cartoon left no doubt that the flow of resources toward Vietnam might starve Johnson’s domestic agenda.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23Vietnam-Lawrence/23Vietnam-Lawrence-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23Vietnam-Lawrence/23Vietnam-Lawrence-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23Vietnam-Lawrence/23Vietnam-Lawrence-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23Vietnam-Lawrence/23Vietnam-Lawrence-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23Vietnam-Lawrence/23Vietnam-Lawrence-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23Vietnam-Lawrence/23Vietnam-Lawrence-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23Vietnam-Lawrence/23Vietnam-Lawrence-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1441, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Vietnam Killed the Great Society', 'kicker': "Vietnam '67", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Lyndon Baines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-23T00:30:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Atwood Lawrence', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': 'Atwood', 'lastname': 'Lawrence', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fb6ea5a-1344-5335-b471-4a2ec3b3b231', 'word_count': 1298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fb6ea5a-1344-5335-b471-4a2ec3b3b231'}, {'abstract': 'When cities rely on private donors, we end up with sterile, controlled environments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/22/opinion/parks-public-spaces-philanthropy.html', 'snippet': 'When cities rely on private donors, we end up with sterile, controlled environments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few years ago, the Hudson River Park Trust floated an idea for a new island. Called Pier 55, it was to be a two-and-a-half-acre wavy rectangle 200 feet off Manhattan reached by two narrow bridges, replacing the remains of Pier 54 near West 14th Street, where the Lusitania used to dock.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23demoncheaux/23demoncheaux-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 525, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23demoncheaux/23demoncheaux-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 525}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23demoncheaux/23demoncheaux-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 896, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23demoncheaux/23demoncheaux-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1792, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23demoncheaux/23demoncheaux-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23demoncheaux/23demoncheaux-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23demoncheaux/23demoncheaux-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Parks Lose Their Playfulness', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Praise of Urban Disorder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diller, Barry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'High Line (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pier 55 (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-23T00:35:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas de Monchaux', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de Monchaux', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4512fd7-bfe5-5f78-9dd1-ba364b7f1167', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4512fd7-bfe5-5f78-9dd1-ba364b7f1167'}, {'abstract': 'Had his lawyers known of his brain disease, the football star Aaron Hernandez might have been able to use it as a defense for killing a friend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/22/opinion/aaron-hernandez-cte.html', 'snippet': 'Had his lawyers known of his brain disease, the football star Aaron Hernandez might have been able to use it as a defense for killing a friend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, we learned that Aaron Hernandez, a former star tight end for the New England Patriots and convicted murderer, had a severe form of the degenerative brain disease chronic traumatic encephalopathy. His mood swings, violent tendencies and memory loss almost certainly resulted from thousands of hits on the football field. Mr. Hernandez’s disease was the worst case doctors had seen in someone so young, his lawyer said.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23tucker/23tucker-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23tucker/23tucker-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23tucker/23tucker-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23tucker/23tucker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23tucker/23tucker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23tucker/23tucker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/23/opinion/23tucker/23tucker-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1459, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is C.T.E. a Defense for Murder?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Is C.T.E. A Defense For Murder?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hernandez, Aaron (1989- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-23T00:42:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. Amy Dillard and Lisa A. Tucker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'Amy', 'lastname': 'Dillard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Tucker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec1e0516-7142-59b2-955b-78d6ba09eab8', 'word_count': 812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec1e0516-7142-59b2-955b-78d6ba09eab8'}, {'abstract': 'In the aftermath of Hurricane Maria, Puerto Ricans were contemplating empty refrigerators, campfire cooking and perhaps wrangling for fresh water.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/22/us/hurricane-maria-puerto-rico-power.html', 'snippet': 'In the aftermath of Hurricane Maria, Puerto Ricans were contemplating empty refrigerators, campfire cooking and perhaps wrangling for fresh water.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN JUAN, P.R. — Two days after Hurricane Maria flattened this island of 3.5 million people, knocking out all its power 