ts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City Thunder', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTHONY, CARMELO', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-23T18:11:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Cacciola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cacciola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8ed7768-e7ea-5534-bdca-8cdacfed7c51', 'word_count': 1226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8ed7768-e7ea-5534-bdca-8cdacfed7c51'}, {'abstract': 'I didn’t know what sisterhood meant anymore. So I went on a feminist history road trip.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/23/opinion/sunday/feminist-history-road-trip.html', 'snippet': 'I didn’t know what sisterhood meant anymore. So I went on a feminist history road trip.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I do not know how to drive. My middle sister, Kerri, learned to drive a few years ago. We are both well into adulthood, but I still rely on her and our oldest sister, Kirsten, for any sort of transportation outside of New York City. So this summer, when we took a road trip, with our mother and Kirsten’s husband and two children, to women’s history sites, I found myself in the slightly disempowering back seat while on a search for empowerment.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24greenidge3/24greenidge3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24greenidge3/24greenidge3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24greenidge3/24greenidge3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24greenidge3/24greenidge3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1343, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24greenidge3/24greenidge3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24greenidge3/24greenidge3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24greenidge3/24greenidge3-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Sisterhood’ Felt Meaningless. So My Sisters and I Got in the Car.', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Sisterhood’ Failed. So We Hit the Road.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Crandall, Prudence, Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery (Historical)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-23T18:30:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kaitlyn Greenidge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kaitlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenidge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d15b040c-9171-53d5-9356-754817186db4', 'word_count': 1996, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d15b040c-9171-53d5-9356-754817186db4'}, {'abstract': 'The fact that “Jew haters” can be targeted isn’t surprising if you understand how the business model works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/23/opinion/sunday/facebook-ad-scandal.html', 'snippet': 'The fact that “Jew haters” can be targeted isn’t surprising if you understand how the business model works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What does it take to advertise on Facebook to people who openly call themselves “Jew haters” and want to know “how to burn Jews”? About $10 and 15 minutes, according to what the investigative nonprofit ProPublica recently uncovered.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24tufekci/24tufekci-articleLarge.gif', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24tufekci/24tufekci-articleLarge.gif', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/24/opinion/sunday/24tufekci/24tufekci-jumbo.gif', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption':