'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dubenko', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73c541ed-dd60-56d2-9be1-57553857b56e', 'word_count': 1314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73c541ed-dd60-56d2-9be1-57553857b56e'}, {'abstract': 'Murray’s Clothbound Cheddar is made from ingredients from New York State and aged for a year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/25/dining/new-york-cheddar-murrays-cheese.html', 'snippet': 'Murray’s Clothbound Cheddar is made from ingredients from New York State and aged for a year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Several years ago, Murray’s Cheese began working with Cornell University to develop an English-style clothbound Cheddar made solely with ingredients from New York State. The classic pale ivory cheese with fruity, citric flavors is now available. Some commercial Cheddars are coated in wax to prevent mold, but using cheesecloth instead allows this Cheddar to develop complex flavors as it ages. The cheese, made in very small batches at the Cornell Food Processing and Development Laboratory in Ithaca, N.Y., is aged for 12 months in Murray’s ripening caves in Queens. The resulting Cheddar has a creamy, yet firm and slightly crumbly texture. The cow milk comes from the Old Chatham Sheepherding Company in the Hudson Valley and, vegetarians should note, the wheels are rubbed with lard from New York State pigs before the cheesecloth is applied, something that is frequently done when making Cheddar: Murray’s Clothbound Cheddar, $29.99 a pound, murrayscheese.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/27/dining/27Burner4/27Burner4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thum