11:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cdc08b3-8eb8-5a3b-9b0d-26e965e4a03d', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cdc08b3-8eb8-5a3b-9b0d-26e965e4a03d'}, {'abstract': '“As athletes, we have a very unique opportunity to speak up about things that we believe in,” the American figure skater Adam Rippon said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/26/sports/olympics/anthem-protests-winter-olympics.html', 'snippet': '“As athletes, we have a very unique opportunity to speak up about things that we believe in,” the American figure skater Adam Rippon said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah — The protesting of racism and police brutality by American athletes started with W.N.B.A. players at a news conference in July 2016 and soon spread to the playing fields of the N.F.L. and the hardwood courts of the N.B.A. In less than five months, the Winter Games in Pyeongchang, South Korea, will present a far larger stage for demonstration: an Olympic podium.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/27/sports/27olympicspic1/27olympicspic1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/27/sports/27olympicspic1/27olympicspic1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/27/sports/27olympicspic1/27olympicspic1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': '