/www.nytimes.com/2017/09/28/theater/sarah-ruhl-for-peter-pan.html', 'snippet': 'Seeing Sarah Ruhl’s “For Peter Pan” reminds a critic of her own father and why she turns to theater to “confront the hard stuff,” like grieving his death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The grown-up kids have gathered at their childhood home, where a white-haired man is puttering around behind them. His name is George, and he is their dad. Just a little bit ago, in the first scene of Sarah Ruhl’s play “For Peter Pan on her 70th birthday,” we watched him die in a hospital bed. Then he sat up, swung his legs over the side and walked away unnoticed, a freshly minted ghost.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/01/arts/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/10/01/arts/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/01/arts/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/01/arts/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/10/01/arts/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/01/arts/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1/01DAUGHTERPLAYS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 