': {'main': 'Carven: Spring 2018', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carven (Fashion Label)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paris Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-28T17:42:56+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': "Women's Runway", 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/883109e1-8be4-50e5-a296-d16c1e977612', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/883109e1-8be4-50e5-a296-d16c1e977612'}, {'abstract': 'Even less likely to work now than then.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/09/28/voodoo-gets-even-voodooier/', 'snippet': 'Even less likely to work now than then.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I liked Binyamin Appelbaum’s historical comparison of the Trump tax cut with Reagan and Bush, although such a comparison risks suggesting that the previous rounds made sense. They didn’t. The Reagan tax cut caused a sharp reversal in the long-run trend toward a declining debt-GDP ratio, the Bush tax cuts undid the fiscal progress of the Clinton years. And conversely, the Clinton tax hike helped produce surpluses even as the economy experienced a large boom, totally the opposite of what the usual suspects on the right predicted.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Voodoo Gets Even Voodooier', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-09-28T17:49:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/072cb9b7-e0da-5ae6-8c8b-32a8eb890f43', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/072cb9b7-e0da-5ae6-8c8b-32a8eb890f43'}, {'abstract': 'Senator Luther Strange, who lost a primary runoff this week, was shadowed by the suspicion that his appointment was part of a deal with the governor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/28/us/alabama-senator-strange.html', 'snippet': 'Senator Luther Strange, who lost a primary runoff this week, was shadowed by the suspicion that his appointment was part of a deal with the governor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTGOMERY, Ala. — The first public whiff of the drama came two years ago, with a surprise divorce filing by the governor’s wife of more than 50 years. A surreptitious, salacious recording surfaced months later. Then, this past April, Gov. Robert Bentley of Alabama, a Republican, resigned under the threat of impeachment over his relationship with a close aide.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/29/us/00alabama-01/00alabama-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/29/us/00alabama-01/00alabama-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/29/us/00alabama-01/00alabama-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/29/us/00alabama-01/00alabama-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlar