': 'Instagram Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-28T19:17:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aparna Nancherla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aparna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nancherla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc8a42e5-325e-57f8-91bf-ec6c457e9e31', 'word_count': 781, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc8a42e5-325e-57f8-91bf-ec6c457e9e31'}, {'abstract': 'The new policy, which officials called an effort to more effectively screen those coming to the country, drew alarm from privacy advocates.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/28/us/politics/immigrants-social-media-trump.html', 'snippet': 'The new policy, which officials called an effort to more effectively screen those coming to the country, drew alarm from privacy advocates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Department of Homeland Security will soon begin collecting social media data from all immigrants entering the United States, part of what agency officials call an effort to more effectively screen those coming to the country but privacy advocates see as an unnecessary intrusion that would do little to protect national security.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/29/us/29dc-dhs/29dc-dhs-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/09/29/us/29dc-dhs/29dc-dhs-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/09/29/us/2