erior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turrell, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larsen, Jack Lenor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-09-30T10:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SpecialSections', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zahid Sardar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zahid', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sardar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b46c91ea-2508-536d-9d1b-cc3d91dadd82', 'word_count': 956, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b46c91ea-2508-536d-9d1b-cc3d91dadd82'}, {'abstract': 'Long gone are the days of party-issued uniforms and uninspired Soviet style. In Moscow and Kazan, the photographer Sasha Arutyunova captured the Russian sartorial ambition to stand out through bold colors, vintage or handmade items and eye-catching patterns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/09/30/style/russia-street-style.html', 'snippet': 'Long gone are the days of party-issued uniforms and uninspired Soviet style. In Moscow and Kazan, the photographer Sasha Arutyunova captured the Russian sartorial ambition to stand out through bold colors, vintage or handmade items and eye-catchin...', 'lead_paragraph': '“I feel really connected to Moscow, but I also still feel a little bit like a tourist,” Sasha Arutyunova said. Ms. Arutyunova, who lives in Brooklyn, was born in Moscow in 1988, a few years before the Soviet Union collapsed and during an era when Levi’s were still a coveted item in the country. She moved to Florida with her mother when she was 6 years old. ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/10/01/fashion/01THELOOK1/01THELOOK1-articl